INSERT INTO public.lore_entries (slug, title, source_url, summary, content, keywords) VALUES
('dragons', 'Dragons', 'everbloom-lore/characters/dragons--6e6aec3d-3d9a-4a59-82c3-0f929d7fb05b.md', 'Ancient, autonomous entities from The Wilderness that act as the world''s Economic Regulators.', 'Dragons

Ancient, autonomous entities from The Wilderness that act as the world''s Economic Regulators.

The Hoard Mechanic
Dragons are attracted to stagnant value.
• If players or cities hoard uncommitted Gold, a Dragon is spawned.
• Behavior: They attack vaults, disrupt trade routes, and consume wealth.

Function
Dragons prevent inflation and hoarding. They are forces of nature, not malice.
• Killing a Dragon: Releases massive amounts of Gold back into the economy (creating inflation/chaos).
• Surviving a Dragon: Requires spending Gold (defense, tribute), thus solving the hoarding problem.', ARRAY['dragons']::text[]),
('the-veiled', 'Followers of the Speaker', 'everbloom-lore/characters/followers-of-the-speaker--35cf3e89-5f9e-45b3-9a24-912574d08728.md', 'The silent priesthood that maintains The First City.', 'Followers of the Speaker (The Veiled)

The silent priesthood that maintains The First City.

Visuals
They cover their mouths with silver cloth or masks to honor the power of Voice.

Doctrine
• Silence: To speak for the divine is to corrupt it.
• Neutrality: They serve the structure of the world, not the Aethers.
• Observation: They record Measure but do not judge it.

Role
They facilitate Character Creation at the City Gate and maintain the Temple of Alignment.', ARRAY['followers of the speaker', 'the veiled']::text[]),
('khalvaris', 'Khalvaris', 'everbloom-lore/characters/khalvaris--361f556d-bc30-4c41-8e63-a3da87761600.md', 'The Judge. The Aether of War and Law.', 'Khalvaris (Wrath / Patience)

The Judge. The Aether of War and Law.
• Domain: The Iron Bastion (A volcanic, fortified land).
• Virtue (Patience): The discipline to wait; strategic defense.
• Sin (Wrath): Unchecked vengeance; scorched earth.
• Followers: Soldiers, Judges, Executioners.
• Wardens: Includes Ardent (The Fiery Judgment) and Verity (The Scales).', ARRAY['khalvaris']::text[]),
('lazareth', 'Lazareth', 'everbloom-lore/characters/lazareth--b4eecf11-7064-4d78-aa5e-7d1a95fd6252.md', 'The Archivist. The Aether of Time and Knowledge.', 'Lazareth (Sloth / Diligence)

The Archivist. The Aether of Time and Knowledge.
• Domain: The Silent Horizon – A vast, crystalline desert where time itself seems to have congealed. Its mountains are petrified waves of sand, its valleys lined with the ruins of civilizations that never were, and scattered through it are libraries built from compressed starlight, holding impossible histories.
• Virtue (Diligence): Deep, unwavering focus; methodical mastery of complex systems; the relentless and unbiased recording of all histories, both triumphant and forgotten. Lazareth''s diligent followers seek out every shard of truth, no matter how small or hidden.
• Sin (Sloth): Apathy born of overwhelming knowledge; a refusal to act due to a perceived futility in the face of inevitable entropy; the deliberate burying or obfuscation of truths that challenge established narratives, allowing silence to swallow vital information.
• Followers: Scholars poring over ancient Tomes and Staffs, historians meticulously chronicling every whispered rumor, and precision sniper-archetypes who believe that a single, perfectly placed shot can alter the course of written destiny. They are often robed in shades of muted grey and indigo, their movements precise and economical.', ARRAY['lazareth']::text[]),
('morvayne', 'Morvayne', 'everbloom-lore/characters/morvayne--b2e0e652-8a00-41fd-96cd-d39034b99213.md', 'The Harvester. The Aether of Nature and Consumption.', 'Morvayne (Gluttony / Temperance)

The Harvester. The Aether of Nature and Consumption.
• Domain: The Feastlands (Overgrown, lush, and predatory).
• Virtue (Temperance): Balance; taking only what you need.
• Sin (Gluttony): Endless hunger; over-consumption; rot.
• Followers: Hunters, Druids, Alchemists.', ARRAY['morvayne']::text[]),
('nyxara', 'Nyxara', 'everbloom-lore/characters/nyxara--eabb3854-6e25-4a22-9247-41cfc4691d3a.md', 'The Muse. The Aether of Ambition and Transformation.', 'Nyxara (Envy / Kindness)

The Muse. The Aether of Ambition and Transformation.
• Domain: The Mirrorlands (A shifting, illusion-filled forest).
• Virtue (Kindness): Empathy; helping others grow.
• Sin (Envy): Coveting power; sabotage; identity theft.
• Followers: Spies, Artists, Mages.
• Note: Often worshipped by Caelen (from the story) as "Bound Wisdom."', ARRAY['nyxara']::text[]),
('self-formed', 'Self-Formed', 'everbloom-lore/characters/self-formed.md', 'Travellers who exist entirely outside the pre-ordained causal logic of The Light and the Aethers. The Aethers fear them as unpredictable variables. Surviving in The Wilderness as one of the Self-Formed is one way into Chaos.', 'Self-Formed

Travellers who exist entirely outside the pre-ordained causal logic of The Light and the Aethers. The Aethers fear them as unpredictable variables. Surviving in The Wilderness as one of the Self-Formed is one way into Chaos.

(Stub: how one becomes Self-Formed is not yet written. The game does not model it yet.)', ARRAY['self-formed', 'self formed']::text[]),
('solastin', 'Solastin', 'everbloom-lore/characters/solastin--7230b2bf-9aa1-49a0-854d-4f1cbb169db3.md', 'The Architect. The Aether of Civilization and Leadership.', 'Solastin (Pride / Humility)

The Architect. The Aether of Civilization and Leadership.
• Domain: The High Sanctum (A kingdom of spires and glass).
• Virtue (Humility): Service to the many; building foundations.
• Sin (Pride): Arrogance; belief that only you can fix the world.
• Followers: Builders, Kings, Leaders.
• Combat Style: Defensive structures, radiant light, command buffs.

The Radiant Aether (in depth)

One of The Seven Aethers, Solastin embodies radiant truth and unyielding justice. Born from the primordial light that pierced the initial void, some say directly from a refraction of The Speaker''s initial creative impulse, Solastin is the eternal sentinel against deception, corruption, and the subtle creeping darkness of Chaos. Their essence is clarity, their will, unwavering law. Solastin is also associated with Pride and Humility, specifically the arrogance that obscures truth versus the humble dedication to its revelation.

Domain
The purview of Solastin extends beyond simple judgment; it encompasses the revelation of truth. They are called upon in oaths, sought for guidance in clouded times, and revered as the ultimate arbiter of cosmic balance. Where mysteries persist and shadows gather, Solastin''s light is believed to eventually find purchase, illuminating all that is hidden. Their domain, often referred to as The High Sanctum, is a Crescent Kingdoms realm of towering spires and reflective surfaces, meticulously designed to capture and amplify natural light, mirroring Solastin''s own illuminating nature.

Appearance
Solastin appears as an incandescent figure, often described as a living sunbeam or a form woven from pure, golden light, without discernible flesh or bone. Their presence is said to bring warmth and pierce shadows, causing those with ill intent to recoil. Unlike the more martial Khalvaris or enigmatic Lazareth, Solastin''s visage is one of pristine, unblemished light.

The Beacon and the Blade
Solastin''s power manifests in two primary ways:
• The Beacon: A pervasive, spiritual light that seeks out falsehoods and offers clarity. It is the inspiration behind many ancient laws and codes of conduct, often found within the doctrines of orders like the Followers of the Speaker. This aspect resonates with Humility, as it guides rather than dictates.
• The Blade: A cutting truth that can excise corruption and bring swift, decisive justice. This aspect is less about mercy and more about the surgical removal of blight, often represented by devastating, purifying blasts of energy that can rival the destructive force of a dragon''s fury when roused. This is where Pride in one''s own righteousness can manifest, leading to uncompromising judgment.

Influence
Cultures dedicated to Solastin often prioritize order, transparency, and logical deduction. Their architecture might feature towering spires and reflective surfaces, designed to capture and amplify natural light. They are often staunch opponents of Chaos, seeing it as the ultimate obfuscation of truth and a direct affront to the structured reality The Speaker articulated. Many paladins and inquisitors across the lands are said to draw their conviction from Solastin''s radiant will, often wielding amplified truths through Tomes and Staffs inscribed with sacred geometry.

Connections
• The Speaker: Solastin''s light is said to originate from The Speaker''s initial creative act, connecting Solastin to the fundamental source of all reality.
• The Seven Aethers: As one of the Aethers, Solastin represents a core virtue (Humility) and sin (Pride) that shapes a fundamental aspect of the world, specifically truth and justice.
• Chaos: Solastin stands in direct opposition to Chaos, embodying clarity and order against the Unbound''s rejection of structure.
• The High Sanctum: This is Solastin''s direct domain, a physical manifestation of their principles of light and structure within The Crescent Kingdoms.
• Followers of the Speaker: The emphasis on truth and ancient laws in Solastin''s domain aligns with the doctrines of the veiled priesthood in The First City.
• Khalvaris: While both Aethers deal with justice, Solastin focuses on revealing truth, while Khalvaris (Wrath/Patience) governs the execution of justice through warfare and law, making them complementary but distinct forces.', ARRAY['solastin']::text[]),
('thalvora', 'Thalvora', 'everbloom-lore/characters/thalvora--7f5e1593-6052-4e2b-9560-4190630198c1.md', 'The Merchant. The Aether of Exchange and Value. A shimmering, ever-shifting entity draped in coin-spun silks, with eyes like polished emeralds that tally every flicker of desire.', 'Thalvora (Greed / Charity)

The Merchant. The Aether of Exchange and Value. A shimmering, ever-shifting entity draped in coin-spun silks, with eyes like polished emeralds that tally every flicker of desire.
• Domain: The Gilded Bazaar – Not merely a place, but a dimension woven from countless markets, bustling souks, and silent vaults. Its air hums with bartering whispers and the chime of falling coin, where every transaction echoes through its shifting alleys and gilded domes.
• Virtue (Charity): The boundless circulation of wealth and resources; the strategic investment that births new industries and fuels societal growth, ensuring no talent or innovation lies fallow.
• Sin (Greed): The stagnant accumulation of capital; the exploitation of want for profit; the insidious belief that gold holds more intrinsic worth than the life it could sustain or the hands that earned it. This sin draws the ire of the Dragons.
• Followers: Astute Merchants, pragmatic Bankers, audacious Thieves, and all who understand that wealth is a current, whether to be channeled or diverted.
• Mechanic: Followers gain significant bonuses to Gold Economy transactions, finding favorable rates, hidden opportunities, and ease in trade. However, their increasing hoards of uncommitted gold attract Dragons proportionally faster, serving as a constant reminder of Thalvora''s judgmental nature toward stagnation.

Connections
Thalvora stands as a counterpoint to Morvayne''s wild consumption, focusing on regulated exchange rather than raw intake. Her domain, the Gilded Bazaar, often touches upon the structured elegance of Solastin''s Lumen Spires for grander financial institutions, yet also brushes against the shadowed alleys where Nyxara''s followers might seek to steal secrets or transform meager beginnings into vast fortunes. The threat of Dragons links her directly to the natural world''s inherent balancing forces.', ARRAY['thalvora']::text[]),
('the-seven-aethers', 'The Seven Aethers', 'everbloom-lore/characters/the-seven-aethers--295ceb16-8a70-4277-b483-79bfef648068.md', 'Elevated intelligences born from The Speaker''s resonant call, they are not gods, but the fundamental architecture of existence itself. Each Aether pulses with a unique frequency, shaping the world through its inherent nature.', 'The Seven Aethers

Elevated intelligences born from The Speaker''s resonant call, they are not gods, but the fundamental architecture of existence itself. Each Aether pulses with a unique frequency, shaping the world through its inherent nature.

The Nature of Aethers
Each Aether embodies a profound Virtue and a corresponding, inescapable Sin. They do not rule through decree or physical force, but by a pervasive Bias, subtly guiding the currents of fate and the instincts of mortals (see Aether Intervention). Their influence is felt in the rustle of leaves, the ambition in a merchant''s heart, or the tremor before a storm.

Mortality
Aethers are functionally immortal, their essences woven into the fabric of reality. However, their continued existence is inextricably linked to their Wardens.
• Each Aether is bound to 12 Wardens, keystones of their spiritual presence.
• Should all 12 Wardens be destroyed, the Aether undergoes a terrifying process of Desynchronization, its essence fraying and tearing until eventual, inevitable oblivion.
• See Warden Destruction Rules for the grim calculus of their demise.

List of Aethers
1.  Khalvaris: The Aether of Justice and Wrath. (Governance: Law, Order, Retribution)
2.  Solastin: The Aether of Pride and Humility. (Governance: Civilization, Structure, Enlightenment)
3.  Nyxara: The Aether of Envy and Kindness. (Governance: Ambition, Transformation, Creation)
4.  Velithria: The Aether of Lust and Chastity. (Governance: Passion, Desire, Purity)
5.  Thalvora: The Aether of Greed and Charity. (Governance: Commerce, Value, Exchange)
6.  Lazareth: The Aether of Sloth and Diligence. (Governance: Knowledge, Time, Memory)
7.  Morvayne: The Aether of Gluttony and Temperance. (Governance: Nature, Sustenance, Consumption)

Relations
The Aethers, in their ancient wisdom, exhibit a deep-seated apprehension towards Self-Formed players. These individuals operate entirely outside the pre-ordained causal logic of the Light and the Aethers'' pervasive influence, representing an unpredictable variable in the carefully balanced tapestry of the world. They are anomalies, ripples in the otherwise predictable currents.

Connections
The distinct domains of the Aethers are often reflected in the varied landscapes of The Crescent Kingdoms, while their clashing ''Biases'' can fuel conflicts or cultural differences between these realms. The very essence of The First City''s neutrality is a testament to its attempt to exist outside the direct influence of any single Aether.', ARRAY['seven aethers', 'the seven aethers']::text[]),
('the-speaker', 'The Speaker', 'everbloom-lore/characters/the-speaker--381ce6eb-e173-481d-b2fd-876a3991ccc3.md', 'The luminous, singular consciousness that burst forth from The Light, its very essence a resonating frequency that spoke reality into being. It is not a form, but a voice, a vibration that defines existence.', 'The Speaker

The luminous, singular consciousness that burst forth from The Light, its very essence a resonating frequency that spoke reality into being. It is not a form, but a voice, a vibration that defines existence.

Role in History
1.  Creation: With the first resonant Word, it wove the fabric of the cosmos, shaping the raw potential of The Light into the tangible world and imbuing it with the fundamental forces of The Seven Aethers.
2.  Withdrawal: After the cosmos took breath, the Speaker receded, its echoes lingering but its direct presence vanishing. This voluntary silence was a profound act, granting the nascent world and its inhabitants the crucible of true free will, untainted by omnipresent guidance.
3.  Legacy: In its parting, fragments of its creative Voice splintered and settled across the world, manifesting as Relics. These aren''t merely artifacts, but echoes, whispers of creation, imbued with a fraction of the Speaker''s world-shaping resonance.

The Experiment
The Speaker''s grand act of creation was underpinned by a solitary, profound inquiry, whispered into the void before its withdrawal:
"What will you become, when you are free to choose?"
The entire tapestry of existence, the struggles of The Seven Aethers, the chaos and order, are all unfolding answers to this singular question.

Relics
Speaker Relics are impossibly potent. They are not merely powerful items, but fundamental keys that can unravel or re-stitch the very laws of reality. Aetherial factions like Solastin often seek them for the potential to perfect their vision of the world, while Chaos—fearing their fundamental power to impose order or rewrite its essence—obsessively hunts to destroy them, believing true freedom lies in the absence of such ultimate dominion. These relics are said to hum with a faint, internal song, a phantom echo of the Speaker''s original utterance.

Connections
The nature of the Speaker''s withdrawal and the experiment it initiated are central to understanding the motivations of all factions, particularly The Seven Aethers and their constant interplay. The Followers of the Speaker maintain a silent reverence, seeking to honor the original act of creation through quiet contemplation rather than direct intervention, reflecting the Speaker''s own withdrawal.', ARRAY['speaker', 'the speaker']::text[]),
('velithria', 'Velithria', 'everbloom-lore/characters/velithria--8fff129d-5f61-406d-9dd9-4b526e9bd7b3.md', 'The Lover. The Aether of Passion and Discipline.', 'Velithria (Lust / Chastity)

The Lover. The Aether of Passion and Discipline.
• Domain: The Evershade (A realm of twilight and sensory overload).
• Virtue (Chastity): Focus; purity of intent; loyalty.
• Sin (Lust): Obsession; loss of control; hedonism.
• Followers: Bards, Duelists, Fanatics.', ARRAY['velithria']::text[]),
('wardens', 'Wardens', 'everbloom-lore/characters/wardens--df93af1a-36da-4f6e-b910-148981b85144.md', 'The keystones that bind each of The Seven Aethers to the world. Every Aether is bound to twelve Wardens. A Warden may be a person, an item or a place.', 'Wardens

The keystones that bind each of The Seven Aethers to the world. Every Aether is bound to twelve Wardens. A Warden may be a person, an item or a place.

The Rule
An Aether lives while any one of its Wardens still stands. When the last falls, the Aether undergoes Desynchronization and falls. See Warden Destruction Rules.

Kinds
• People: sworn guardians (gate-wardens, masters, road-wardens). A slain Warden does not return.
• Items: relics that rest in a sacred room, such as the Oathstone of Illumination.
• Places: sites that are themselves sacred to the Aether, such as Ardent, the Fiery Judgment of Khalvaris.

The Twelve of Each Aether
• Wardens of Solastin
• Wardens of Khalvaris
• Wardens of Nyxara
• Wardens of Thalvora
• Wardens of Lazareth
• Wardens of Velithria
• Wardens of Morvayne

Connections
The Crescent Kingdoms are defended by the Wardens hidden within their lands. Chaos hunts them.', ARRAY['wardens']::text[]),
('wardens-of-khalvaris', 'Wardens of Khalvaris', 'everbloom-lore/characters/wardens-of-khalvaris.md', 'The twelve Wardens that bind Khalvaris to the world. While any one of them stands, Khalvaris lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).', 'Wardens of Khalvaris

The twelve Wardens that bind Khalvaris to the world. While any one of them stands, Khalvaris lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).

• 1 — Verdict Tarn, First Warden of Khalvaris — person — Iron Bastion — A black-clad judge whose ruling has never been overturned.
• 2 — Bailiff Korr, Warden of the Bastion Gate — person — Bastion Gate — A heavy-shouldered bailiff who searches every cart at the gate.
• 3 — Forge-Magistrate Vey, Warden of the Foundry — person — Foundry Stair — A magistrate who hears cases over the ring of hammers.
• 4 — Verity, the Scales — item — Dueling Pit — The Scales of Khalvaris. Every verdict in the Bastion is weighed on them; while they balance, the Judge endures. Destroyed by: shatter (STR). Tended by Pit-Master Gren, Pit-Master.
• 5 — Helmsman Brod, Warden of Helm Walk — person — Helm Walk — A retired guardsman who keeps a private watch on the wall walk.
• 6 — Forge-Sergeant Asra, Warden of the Forge Rise — person — Forge Rise — A barrel-chested sergeant who drills the road-watch.
• 7 — Cinder-Watch Tel, Warden of the Cinder Mile — person — Cinder Mile — A road-warden whose cloak is permanently grey with ash.
• 8 — Slag-Captain Yorrin, Warden of the Slag Mile — person — Slag Mile — A captain who drills the slag-haulers like a militia.
• 9 — Quench-Marshal Ren, Warden of the Slag Flats — person — Slag Flats — A marshal who keeps the cooling-yards orderly.
• 10 — Iron-Rider Hass, Warden of the Ironroad — person — Ironroad — A road-rider in chain whose horse is shod in iron.
• 11 — Smith-Sworn Brennig, Warden of Anvilrest — person — Anvilrest Square — A sworn smith of the bastion who runs the anvil-spur.
• 12 — Ardent, the Fiery Judgment — place — The Magma Forge — The open furnace where the Bastion''s judgments are tempered. The fire is a Warden: it has never gone out. Destroyed by: desecrate (WIS). Tended by Verdict Steps Korin, Judge''s Second of the Magma Forge.', ARRAY['wardens of khalvaris']::text[]),
('wardens-of-lazareth', 'Wardens of Lazareth', 'everbloom-lore/characters/wardens-of-lazareth.md', 'The twelve Wardens that bind Lazareth to the world. While any one of them stands, Lazareth lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).', 'Wardens of Lazareth

The twelve Wardens that bind Lazareth to the world. While any one of them stands, Lazareth lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).

• 1 — Hush-Master Velen, First Warden of Lazareth — person — Silent Horizon — A pale, soft-voiced master of the great hush.
• 2 — Gate-Hush Ari, Warden of the Horizon Gate — person — Horizon Gate — A gate-warden who waves travelers in without speaking.
• 3 — Stair-Keeper Imo, Warden of the Hush Stair — person — Hush Stair — A stair-keeper whose footfalls cannot be heard.
• 4 — The Glyph Hall — place — Glyph Hall — A long hall whose walls record glyphs from every dead language. While its record holds, Lazareth remembers. Destroyed by: desecrate (WIS). Tended by Glyph-Master Ena, Glyph-Master of the Hall.
• 5 — The Starlight Codex — item — Indexers Hall — A codex bound in compressed starlight that holds every history, including the ones that never were. Destroyed by: shatter (STR). Tended by Indexer Olrik, Senior Indexer.
• 6 — Pine-Walker Sera, Warden of the Hush Pines — person — Hush Pines — A road-warden whose path winds between the black pines.
• 7 — Archive-Rider Tov, Warden of the Archive Rise — person — Archive Rise — A messenger who carries unopened letters along the road.
• 8 — Ink-Watch Mae, Warden of the Ink Mile — person — Ink Mile — A road-warden whose lantern burns black-flame ink.
• 9 — Hush-Watch Riv, Warden of the Hush Mile — person — Hush Mile — A road-warden who has not spoken aloud in nine years.
• 10 — Hush-Master Olen, Warden of the Hush Room — person — The Hush Room — An old master of the inner hush.
• 11 — Pool-Keeper Aen, Warden of the Ink-Dark Pool — person — Ink Dark Pool — A keeper who reads the pool''s surface for unspoken news.
• 12 — Marginalia-Sworn Fen, Warden of Marginalia — person — Marginalia Square — A scribe sworn to the silent archive at the spur.', ARRAY['wardens of lazareth']::text[]),
('wardens-of-morvayne', 'Wardens of Morvayne', 'everbloom-lore/characters/wardens-of-morvayne.md', 'The twelve Wardens that bind Morvayne to the world. While any one of them stands, Morvayne lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).', 'Wardens of Morvayne

The twelve Wardens that bind Morvayne to the world. While any one of them stands, Morvayne lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).

• 1 — Harvest-Master Brun, First Warden of Morvayne — person — Feastlands Keep — The chief harvest-master who plans the feast.
• 2 — Gate-Keeper Wenna, Warden of the Keep Gate — person — Keep Gate — A round-faced gate-keeper who hands out warm bread to children.
• 3 — The Seed of the First Harvest — item — Granary Stair — A single seed from the first harvest, kept in the granary. Temperance says it must never be eaten. Destroyed by: shatter (STR). Tended by Granary-Master Olbi, Granary-Master.
• 4 — The Endless Table — place — Feast Throne — The great table of the Feastlands, which is never cleared. Morvayne''s bounty is laid here. Destroyed by: desecrate (WIS). Tended by Feast-Throne Watch, Steward of the Endless Table.
• 5 — Honey-Loft Keeper, Warden of the Honey Loft — person — Honey Loft — A loft-keeper whose hands are permanently sticky.
• 6 — Furnace-Master Ged, Warden of the Fat Furnace — person — Fat Furnace — A furnace-master who renders the great fats.
• 7 — Hearth-Watch Mira, Warden of the Hearth Road — person — Hearth Road — A road-warden whose lantern burns scented oil.
• 8 — Grain-Mile Watch, Warden of the Grain Mile — person — Grain Mile — A road-warden of the long grain road.
• 9 — Orchard-Mile Watch, Warden of the Orchard Mile — person — Orchard Mile — A road-warden between the orchards.
• 10 — Harvest-Rise Watch, Warden of the Harvest Rise — person — Harvest Rise — A road-warden of the rise.
• 11 — Barley-Flats Watch, Warden of the Barley Flats — person — Barley Flats — A road-warden of the flats.
• 12 — Cider-Hollow-Sworn Pel, Warden of Cider Hollow — person — Cider Hollow Square — A sworn smallholder of the keep at the orchard spur.', ARRAY['wardens of morvayne']::text[]),
('wardens-of-nyxara', 'Wardens of Nyxara', 'everbloom-lore/characters/wardens-of-nyxara.md', 'The twelve Wardens that bind Nyxara to the world. While any one of them stands, Nyxara lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).', 'Wardens of Nyxara

The twelve Wardens that bind Nyxara to the world. While any one of them stands, Nyxara lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).

• 1 — Archivist-Mirror Ish, First Warden of Nyxara — person — Mirrorlands Court — The chief archivist whose hands open every locked memory.
• 2 — Gate-Mirror Tyr, Warden of the Mirror Gate — person — Mirror Gate — A gate-warden who carries a small hand-mirror with him.
• 3 — Walk-Master Ven, Warden of the Mirror Walk — person — Mirror Walk — An old master of the long mirror walk.
• 4 — The Thousandth Mask — item — The Mirror Vault — A mask that has been every face its wearer envied. It waits in the vault for someone to become. Destroyed by: shatter (STR). Tended by Vault-Master Aen, Vault-Master of the Mirror Vault.
• 5 — Chronoglass Ot, Warden of the Observatory — person — Chronoglass Observatory — A keeper of the great glass that records yesterday.
• 6 — Glassmere Warden Pel, Warden of Glassmere — person — Glassmere — A road-warden of the lake hamlet.
• 7 — Reflection-Master Sar, Warden of Reflection Rise — person — Reflection Rise — A master of the rise where the road first sees its own past.
• 8 — Glass-Mile Watch Tar, Warden of the Glass Mile — person — Glass Mile — A road-warden whose lantern is a single curved sheet of glass.
• 9 — Veil-Mile Watch Bren, Warden of the Veil Mile — person — Veil Mile — A road-warden in a long pale veil.
• 10 — The Quiet Mirror — place — Quiet Mirror — A single mirror the size of a wall that shows who you could become, and refuses some viewers. Nyxara looks out through it. Destroyed by: desecrate (WIS). Tended by Quiet-Mirror Keeper, Keeper of the Quiet Mirror.
• 11 — Mirror-Pass Watch Lor, Warden of the Mirror Pass — person — Mirror Pass — A road-warden of the high pass into the Mirrorlands.
• 12 — Quietshore-Sworn Inara, Warden of Quietshore — person — Quietshore Square — A glassblower sworn to the Mirrorlands at the lake spur.', ARRAY['wardens of nyxara']::text[]),
('wardens-of-solastin', 'Wardens of Solastin', 'everbloom-lore/characters/wardens-of-solastin.md', 'The twelve Wardens that bind Solastin to the world. While any one of them stands, Solastin lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).', 'Wardens of Solastin

The twelve Wardens that bind Solastin to the world. While any one of them stands, Solastin lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).

• 1 — Theron Sun-Marshal, First Warden of Solastin — person — High Sanctum — A grey-haired knight whose bare blade is said to keep the Beacon lit.
• 2 — Sister Halia, Warden of the Pyre — person — Pyre Court — A pyre-keeper who tends the great fire night and day.
• 3 — The Oathstone of Illumination — item — Oratory of Flame — A crystalline monolith said to be a primordial shard of pure light, gifted by Solastin. Oaths sworn on it bind; lies spoken before it are met with silence or a blinding flash. Destroyed by: shatter (STR). Tended by Brother Cael, Keeper of the Oathstone.
• 4 — The Penitents'' Stair — place — Penitents Stair — The stair the Beacon counts, step by step, each dawn. Every footfall of humility laid here holds up Solastin''s light. Destroyed by: desecrate (WIS). Tended by Penitent Vren, Penitent of the Stair.
• 5 — Ferdan Lance-Rider, Warden of the Causeway — person — Pilgrim Causeway — A horse-warden who escorts pilgrims along the road.
• 6 — Mira of the Sun-Mile, Warden of the Sun-Mile — person — Sun Blanched Mile — A scout whose voice carries from horizon to horizon.
• 7 — Ash Rider, Warden of the Pilgrim Rise — person — Pilgrim Rise — A rider whose horse has worn three sets of shoes on this rise.
• 8 — Kindler Ott, Warden of the Ember Mile — person — Ember Mile — A taciturn kindler who lights the road-fires at dusk.
• 9 — Sun-Squire Bel, Warden of the Foothills — person — Solar Foothills — A young squire in dented gold plate.
• 10 — Smith Roen, Warden of the Solar Forge — person — Solar Forge — A smith who forges only blades meant to catch the sun.
• 11 — Magma-Keeper Dorn, Warden of the Magma Forge — person — The Magma Forge — A scarred old smith who tends the deeper forge.
• 12 — Lantern-Keeper Selene, Warden of the Sanctum Gate — person — Sanctum Gate — A quiet woman who keeps the great lantern at the gate.', ARRAY['wardens of solastin']::text[]),
('wardens-of-thalvora', 'Wardens of Thalvora', 'everbloom-lore/characters/wardens-of-thalvora.md', 'The twelve Wardens that bind Thalvora to the world. While any one of them stands, Thalvora lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).', 'Wardens of Thalvora

The twelve Wardens that bind Thalvora to the world. While any one of them stands, Thalvora lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).

• 1 — Reckoner Vex, First Warden of Thalvora — person — Gilded Bazaar — The chief auditor of the bazaar. Her ledger has no errors.
• 2 — Customs-Master Lora, Warden of the Bazaar Gate — person — Bazaar Gate — A bright, exact customs officer who never accepts a bribe.
• 3 — Bailiff Tov, Warden of the Customs Walk — person — Customs Walk — A bailiff who polices the customs lane.
• 4 — Beggar-Captain Rin, Warden of the Beggars'' Stair — person — Beggars Stair — A beggar-king who keeps order on the lowest tier.
• 5 — The First Coin — item — Coin Press — The first coin ever struck in the Bazaar, kept turning in the great press. Wealth that never stops moving. Destroyed by: shatter (STR). Tended by Pressman Coy, Pressman.
• 6 — Auctioneer Sel, Warden of the Auction Tier — person — Auction Tier — A fast-talking auctioneer who runs the high tier.
• 7 — Stall-Boss Hena, Warden of Black-Stall Row — person — Black Stall Row — A stall-boss who decides which traders work the black row.
• 8 — Debt-Marshal Olwen, Warden of the Debt Pen — person — Debt Pen — A marshal who keeps the debtors orderly until they pay.
• 9 — Underbazaar Boss Trik, Warden of the Underbazaar — person — Underbazaar — A wiry boss who keeps the under-market civil.
• 10 — The Vault of Flowing Coin — place — Vault of Flowing Coin — A vault where gold is never allowed to rest. Thalvora''s charity flows out of it; hoarding here would wake dragons. Destroyed by: desecrate (WIS). Tended by Vault-Keeper Fenn, Keeper of the Vault.
• 11 — River-Toll Mara, Warden of the Coin River — person — Coin Choked River — A toll-keeper at the coin river.
• 12 — Goldwash-Master Pell, Warden of Goldwash — person — Goldwash — A road-master at the goldwash road.', ARRAY['wardens of thalvora']::text[]),
('wardens-of-velithria', 'Wardens of Velithria', 'everbloom-lore/characters/wardens-of-velithria.md', 'The twelve Wardens that bind Velithria to the world. While any one of them stands, Velithria lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).', 'Wardens of Velithria

The twelve Wardens that bind Velithria to the world. While any one of them stands, Velithria lives (see Warden Destruction Rules).

• 1 — Vow-Master Ilen, First Warden of Velithria — person — Evershade Court — The chief vow-keeper who blesses the great promises.
• 2 — Gate-Vow Ren, Warden of the Vow Gate — person — Vow Gate — A gate-warden who lets only the vowed pass at night.
• 3 — Walk-Master Vel, Warden of the Vow Walk — person — Vow Walk — A master of the slow vow walk.
• 4 — The Ribbon of First Vows — item — Candlelit Vow Hall — The ribbon tied around the first vow ever spoken in the Evershade. It has never been untied. Destroyed by: shatter (STR). Tended by Hall-Keeper Mir, Hall-Keeper of the Vow Hall.
• 5 — The Tear-Glass Window — place — Tear Glass Window — A window of set tears whose colors are wrong and beautiful. Every pure vow made beneath it strengthens Velithria. Destroyed by: desecrate (WIS). Tended by Window-Keeper Sela, Keeper of the Tear-Glass.
• 6 — Mourners'' Tier Keeper, Warden of the Mourners'' Tier — person — Mourners Tier — A keeper of the mourners'' tier.
• 7 — Garden-Keeper Faela, Warden of the Veil Garden — person — Veil Garden — A garden-keeper who tends the veiled flowers.
• 8 — Pines-Master Onel, Warden of the Silver Pines — person — Silver Pines — A road-warden of the pines.
• 9 — Mourner Rise Watch, Warden of Mourner Rise — person — Mourner Rise — A road-warden of the mourner''s rise.
• 10 — Silver-Mile Watch, Warden of the Silver Mile — person — Silver Mile — A road-warden of the long silver mile.
• 11 — Vigil-Mile Keeper, Warden of the Vigil Mile — person — Vigil Mile — A road-warden who keeps the night vigil.
• 12 — Vow-Walk Road Watch, Warden of the Vow-Walk Road — person — Vow Walk Road — A road-warden of the long approach.', ARRAY['wardens of velithria']::text[]),
('chaos', 'Chaos', 'everbloom-lore/factions/chaos--ac229061-186c-4c4a-a56e-7b2d1b44fb49.md', 'Chaos is not a faction; it is the refusal of alignment. It is discovered by players who reject the Aethers and The Light.', 'Chaos (The Unbound)

Chaos is not a faction; it is the refusal of alignment. It is discovered by players who reject the Aethers and The Light.

Induction
Players enter Chaos by:
1.  Refusing to choose an Aether.
2.  Speaking the phrase "Non Serviam".
3.  Surviving in The Wilderness as a Self-Formed entity.

Mechanics
• Economy: Chaos players can use Gold but often destabilize markets, attracting Dragons.
• Measure: Chaos players generate high, volatile Measure.
• Death: Chaos players do not have Borrowed Immortality. If they die, their character is deleted permanently. See Death and Identity.', ARRAY['chaos']::text[]),
('the-grand-luminares', 'The Grand Luminares', 'everbloom-lore/factions/the-grand-luminares--d43a08f1-001b-40f3-a5b0-49f7363ebb17.md', 'A monastic order dedicated to the unwavering pursuit of truth and the eradication of falsehoods, the Grand Luminares are the foremost interpreters of Solastin''s divine will. Founded in the earliest days of The Crescent Kingdoms, their austere monasteries, known as Lumen Spires, a', 'The Grand Luminares

A monastic order dedicated to the unwavering pursuit of truth and the eradication of falsehoods, the Grand Luminares are the foremost interpreters of Solastin''s divine will. Founded in the earliest days of The Crescent Kingdoms, their austere monasteries, known as Lumen Spires, are scattered throughout the realm, often built atop natural high points or significant ley lines. 

Members, often called Luminars, undergo rigorous training in logic, philosophical debate, and the discernment of subtle deceptions. While generally benevolent, their pursuit of absolute truth can sometimes lead to rigid dogma and an unyielding stance against anything perceived as ''unverifiable,'' occasionally clashing with the more pragmatic approaches of other factions. They are responsible for maintaining the sanctity of the Oathstone of Illumination.', ARRAY['grand luminares', 'the grand luminares', 'religious order', 'solastin', 'crecent kingdoms']::text[]),
('the-light', 'The Light', 'everbloom-lore/factions/the-light--d61706b8-aa20-4e8e-a6f6-d06fac198320.md', 'The primordial force of order and possibility that existed before the world. It is not a god, but a principle.', 'The Light

The primordial force of order and possibility that existed before the world. It is not a god, but a principle.

Function
• Guidance: The Light offers direction to players via subtle cues (glowing paths, wind, intuition).
• The Prism: Located in The First City, it acts as the primary interface for Light-aligned players.
• Philosophy: The Light does not command; it invites. It seeks to maintain the experiment of creation.

Player Alignment
Players who follow the Light:
• Receive Orientation (quests, waypoints).
• Do not gain raw power but gain Trust from systems and NPCs.
• Act as "Anchors" against Chaos.', ARRAY['light', 'the light']::text[]),
('oathstone-of-illumination', 'Oathstone of Illumination', 'everbloom-lore/items/oathstone-of-illumination--be857407-a6bf-4e59-980a-5040aa5373d5.md', 'Within the grandest of the Lumen Spires, nestled deep within The High Sanctum, lies the Oathstone of Illumination. This immense, crystalline monolith is said to be a primordial shard of pure light, gifted by Solastin themselves to guide mortals towards truth. Engraved with ancien', 'Oathstone of Illumination

Within the grandest of the Lumen Spires, nestled deep within The High Sanctum, lies the Oathstone of Illumination. This immense, crystalline monolith is said to be a primordial shard of pure light, gifted by Solastin themselves to guide mortals towards truth. Engraved with ancient, ever-shifting symbols of clarity and law, the Oathstone hums with a faint, resonant energy. 

Those who swear oaths upon its surface are said to be bound by an unbreakable spiritual contract, as the Stone itself can discern intent. Falsehoods spoken in its presence are met with a chilling silence or, for grave deceptions, a sudden, searing flash of light that temporarily blinds the perjurer. It is a sacred artifact, revered by the Grand Luminares and occasionally sought by high-ranking officials of The Crescent Kingdoms for particularly sensitive treaties or judicial proceedings.', ARRAY['oathstone of illumination', 'artifact', 'ritual item', 'solastin']::text[]),
('tomes-and-staffs', 'Tomes and Staffs', 'everbloom-lore/items/tomes-and-staffs--4064882c-401d-4a7d-bfb3-017f4244615a.md', 'Since players cannot "hold" magic in their hands (they are just travelers), they need conduits.', 'Tomes and Staffs (The Amplifiers)

Since players cannot "hold" magic in their hands (they are just travelers), they need conduits.

Staffs (The Projectors)
• Function: A staff is a "Barrel" for a spell.
• Engraving: You engrave the "Trigger" rune at the top and "Shape" runes down the shaft.
• Example:
• Top: "Fire"
• Shaft: "Distance" + "Explode"
• Result: Fireball.
• Customization: A longer staff allows for more modifier runes (Distance, Split, Homing), but takes longer to cast.

Tomes (The Processors)
• Function: A Tome is a "Circuit Board." It holds complex logic spells that run passively.
• Engraving: Pages are made of thin metal or treated leather that must be scribed.
• Usage:
• Page 1: "If Hit -> Cast Shield."
• Page 2: "If Low Health -> Cast Heal."
• Limit: Tomes are heavy and occupy the "Off-hand" slot. Opening a Tome exposes it to damage (pages can be ripped/destroyed).', ARRAY['tomes and staffs']::text[]),
('lumen-spires', 'Lumen Spires', 'everbloom-lore/locations/lumen-spires--91ac1016-ff09-43ef-9279-ec687d7a2d9a.md', 'These towering, often crystal-infused structures serve as both monasteries and bastions of the Grand Luminares. Each Spire is meticulously designed to amplify natural light, with complex arrays of polished lenses and reflective surfaces that channel solar and lunar rays into thei', 'Lumen Spires

These towering, often crystal-infused structures serve as both monasteries and bastions of the Grand Luminares. Each Spire is meticulously designed to amplify natural light, with complex arrays of polished lenses and reflective surfaces that channel solar and lunar rays into their central meditation chambers, known as the ''Truth-Glow Core.'' It is here that Luminars believe the purest essence of Solastin''s radiance can be accessed for contemplation and guidance. 

Many of the Lumen Spires contain ancient libraries of illuminated texts and are built around unique geological formations rumored to possess inherent clarifying properties, such as the Oathstone of Illumination found within the highest Spire of The High Sanctum. They are focal points for those seeking honesty and clarity within The Crescent Kingdoms.', ARRAY['lumen spires', 'monastery', 'solastin', 'architecture', 'crecent kingdoms']::text[]),
('silent-horizon', 'Silent Horizon', 'everbloom-lore/locations/silent-horizon.md', 'Lazareth''s kingdom in The Crescent Kingdoms: a vast crystalline desert where time itself seems to have congealed. Its mountains are petrified waves of sand, its valleys lined with ruins of civilizations that never were, and its libraries are built from compressed starlight and ca', 'Silent Horizon

Lazareth''s kingdom in The Crescent Kingdoms: a vast crystalline desert where time itself seems to have congealed. Its mountains are petrified waves of sand, its valleys lined with ruins of civilizations that never were, and its libraries are built from compressed starlight and carved into canyons whose entrances are guarded by petrified scholars.
• Ruler: Lazareth, the Archivist.
• Seat: the Archive Throne.
• Notable: the Glyph Hall, whose walls record every dead language; the Indexers'' Hall; the Hush Stair.
• Wardens: see Wardens of Lazareth.', ARRAY['silent horizon']::text[]),
('crescent-kingdoms', 'The Crescent Kingdoms', 'everbloom-lore/locations/the-crescent-kingdoms--55ada5be-be91-4fb2-8684-fd7f877b536a.md', 'The seven civilized realms that form a buffer between The First City and The Wilderness. Their collective form describes a loose arc, a bastion of structured existence against the unbound wilds.', 'The Crescent Kingdoms

The seven civilized realms that form a buffer between The First City and The Wilderness. Their collective form describes a loose arc, a bastion of structured existence against the unbound wilds.

Structure
• Each Kingdom is ruled by one of The Seven Aethers, their chosen vice or virtue echoing across the land itself.
• Geography: Shaped dramatically by the presiding Aether''s domain. For example:
• Khalvaris''s realm, The Iron Bastion, is a land of obsidian peaks and sulfuric springs, where fortresses rise like clenched fists from the volatile earth.
• Solastin''s High Sanctum boasts crystalline spires that touch the clouds, with rivers flowing like liquid light through impossibly symmetrical cities.
• Lazareth''s Silent Horizon is a vast, arid expanse where time seems to slow, ancient libraries built into desert canyons, their entrances guarded by petrified scholars.
• Thalvora''s Gilded Bazaar is a kaleidoscopic maze of merchant tents, floating markets on silken rivers, and towering bazaars, where every stone seems to shimmer with stored value.
• Morvayne''s Feastlands are an overture of verdant excess, jungles dripping with fruit and predator alike, where nature reclaims civilization with a hungry embrace.
• Nyxara''s Mirrorlands are a chimerical landscape of shimmering illusions, mist-shrouded forests where paths shift and reflections deceive.
• Velithria''s Evershade is a twilight realm of intoxicating sensory overload, where perfumed breezes carry whispered secrets through blossoming, bioluminescent gardens.

Stability
The Kingdoms are stable but stagnant, held in a delicate, almost ritualistic balance. They rely on Institutional Inertia to survive, a slow grind against the encroaching chaos.
• Borders: Rarely change, hardened by ancient pacts and the lingering scars of the Crescent Wars. Alteration usually requires cataclysmic intervention, often heralded by the awakening of Dragons or a shift in Aetheric influence.
• Defense: Protected by the 12 Wardens hidden within their lands—ancient, powerful beings bound by oaths to repel existential threats to the Aethers'' balance. Each Warden embodies an aspect of defense, from unbreakable shields to the subtle art of misdirection.

Connections
• The Speaker: The ultimate source from which the Aethers and their domains sprung, though now withdrawn.
• Followers of the Speaker: Their doctrine of silence in The First City contrasts sharply with the manifest expressions of the Aethers in the Kingdoms.
• The Wilderness: The untamed, unpredictable counterpart to the structured order of the Kingdoms, acting as a constant pressure.
• Chaos: The ultimate antithesis to the Aethers'' structured domains, a force that seeks to unravel the very nature of the Crescent Kingdoms.
• Prehistory: The Kingdoms’ current state is a direct consequence of the Crescent Wars, which defined their borders and solidified Aetheric influence.', ARRAY['crescent kingdoms']::text[]),
('first-city', 'The First City', 'everbloom-lore/locations/the-first-city--5a9ad6bc-4fd5-4bfd-95fc-dc9b815fe3d7.md', 'The neutral convergence point of the world. It acts as an interface between the Wilderness and The Crescent Kingdoms.', 'The First City

The neutral convergence point of the world. It acts as an interface between the Wilderness and The Crescent Kingdoms.

Governance
It is maintained, not ruled, by the Followers of the Speaker (The Veiled). No Aether holds dominion here, ensuring free choice.

Key Locations
• The Gate of Form: Where Character Creation occurs for those seeking recognition.
• The Hall of Seven Ways: The chamber where players choose their Alignment.
• The Hidden Fissure: A secret location where the phrase "Non Serviam" can be spoken to discover Chaos.

Economy
The city is the primary ledger for Measure. It is also the safest place to store Gold before it is committed to the world.', ARRAY['first city']::text[]),
('the-high-sanctum', 'The High Sanctum', 'everbloom-lore/locations/the-high-sanctum.md', 'Solastin''s kingdom in The Crescent Kingdoms: crystalline spires that touch the clouds, rivers flowing like liquid light through impossibly symmetrical cities. Its towers are built to capture and amplify natural light.', 'The High Sanctum

Solastin''s kingdom in The Crescent Kingdoms: crystalline spires that touch the clouds, rivers flowing like liquid light through impossibly symmetrical cities. Its towers are built to capture and amplify natural light.
• Ruler: Solastin, the Architect.
• Seat: the Beacon Throne, above the Sanctum.
• Notable: the Lumen Spires of the Grand Luminares; the Oathstone of Illumination.
• Wardens: see Wardens of Solastin.', ARRAY['high sanctum', 'the high sanctum']::text[]),
('the-wilderness', 'The Wilderness', 'everbloom-lore/locations/the-wilderness.md', 'The untamed, unpredictable land beyond The Crescent Kingdoms, and the constant pressure against their order. Dragons come from here. Those who survive here outside any alignment may become Self-Formed.', 'The Wilderness

The untamed, unpredictable land beyond The Crescent Kingdoms, and the constant pressure against their order. Dragons come from here. Those who survive here outside any alignment may become Self-Formed.

In the game: the roads, wilds, deserts, groves and shores between towns, where random encounters, camping ambushes and the deep-desert wander happen.

(Stub: the owner has not yet written this page in full.)', ARRAY['wilderness', 'the wilderness']::text[]),
('aether-intervention', 'Aether Intervention', 'everbloom-lore/lore/aether-intervention.md', 'The Aethers do not rule by force but by bias. In their own kingdoms that bias is close to rule: the people of a kingdom live under its Aether.', 'Aether Intervention

The Aethers do not rule by force but by bias. In their own kingdoms that bias is close to rule: the people of a kingdom live under its Aether.

In turn, each living Aether wakes, looks over its kingdom and issues decrees to its people: goals for particular subjects, laws for a season, rumours, festivals, bonds between people. Its proclamations are heard at its throne and capital.

Otherworlders are free. An Aether can only nudge them: summons and invitations sent to those sworn to it, never commands.', ARRAY['aether intervention']::text[]),
('alignment', 'Alignment', 'everbloom-lore/lore/alignment.md', 'In the Hall of Seven Ways in The First City, every traveller chooses.', 'Alignment

In the Hall of Seven Ways in The First City, every traveller chooses.
• Swear to an Aether. Kneel and bind yourself to one of The Seven Aethers. One-time. The sworn walk a seven-step vow chain for their Aether, are carried home by it at death while it lives (see Borrowed Immortality), and may receive its nudges (an Aether rules its NPCs, but cannot command a player).
• Walk out. Refusing to choose is remembered: each refusal adds chaos pressure, and the Shadow''s whispers grow (see The Shadow).
• Non Serviam. Forsake all seven and join Chaos. Irreversible.

Followers of The Light receive orientation and trust rather than power.', ARRAY['alignment']::text[]),
('borrowed-immortality', 'Borrowed Immortality', 'everbloom-lore/lore/borrowed-immortality.md', 'The sworn do not truly die while their Aether lives. When a traveller sworn to a living Aether falls, that Aether carries them to its city well, alive at half strength.', 'Borrowed Immortality

The sworn do not truly die while their Aether lives. When a traveller sworn to a living Aether falls, that Aether carries them to its city well, alive at half strength.
• If their Aether has fallen, it can only carry them home stripped of coin and gear.
• Those with no Aether (and all of Chaos) have no borrowed immortality. See Death and Identity.', ARRAY['borrowed immortality']::text[]),
('character-creation', 'Character Creation', 'everbloom-lore/lore/character-creation.md', 'Happens at the Gate of Form of The First City, facilitated by the Veiled.', 'Character Creation

Happens at the Gate of Form of The First City, facilitated by the Veiled.

A newcomer chooses:
• a name and a short description of themselves (the Veiled see them as they are: a humble traveller in plain clothes);
• a race: see Races;
• a class: see Classes.

Their body is shaped by both: hit points and six ability scores (Strength, Dexterity, Constitution, Intelligence, Wisdom, Charisma) start at 10, raised or lowered by race and class. Every uncertain deed in the world is a dice roll plus these scores.

Next, they choose their Alignment.', ARRAY['character creation']::text[]),
('classes', 'Classes', 'everbloom-lore/lore/classes.md', 'The callings an Otherworlder may take up at the Gate of Form (see Character Creation). Each raises two ability scores; at level 15 a classed traveller may Awaken into one of its evolutions.', 'Classes

The callings an Otherworlder may take up at the Gate of Form (see Character Creation). Each raises two ability scores; at level 15 a classed traveller may Awaken into one of its evolutions.

Warrior
steel, sweat, and the last stand

You learned young that a well-placed blade ends more arguments than any prayer. The road remembers your footfalls.
• Abilities: STR +2, CON +1; HP +3.

Mage
grammar of the unspoken

The world has verbs older than any tongue. You have learned three of them and lived.
• Abilities: INT +2, WIS +1; HP -1.

Monk
still water, sudden strike

You need no weapon but your breath. Your fists are a philosophy the world does not want.
• Abilities: DEX +2, WIS +1; HP +1.

Paladin
a vow with edges

Your god is your weapon. Or your weapon is your god — you have stopped counting the difference.
• Abilities: STR +2, CHA +1; HP +2.

Ranger
quiet feet, quiet arrows

The road knows you better than any city ever did. You know the road better than it knows itself.
• Abilities: DEX +2, WIS +1; HP +1.

Rogue
nobody sees you leave

Everything has a lock. Every lock has a key. You have decided you are the key.
• Abilities: DEX +2, INT +1; HP +0.

Summoner
you were never alone

The names in your throat are older than your teeth. When you call, they still come.
• Abilities: CHA +2, INT +1; HP -1.

Gambler
luck is a muscle

You have folded better hands than most people ever hold. Every road is a coin, and you have learned to feel it turning.
• Abilities: CHA +2, DEX +1; HP +0.

Bard
a song they cannot unhear

You remember every name, every wound, every debt. Your voice does the work three swords could not.
• Abilities: CHA +2, DEX +1; HP +0.

Alchemist
salt, ash, and stubbornness

Every liquid is a promise. You have learned which promises are worth keeping and which will kill you slowly.
• Abilities: INT +2, CON +1; HP +0.

Necromancer
the dead still owe you

Death did not stop you. It only slowed you down. You have taken it as a partner.
• Abilities: INT +2, WIS +1; HP -1.

Runewright
the letter is the sword

You have learned that a well-cut sigil holds more than any blade. Ink is your discipline; stone is your page.
• Abilities: INT +2, WIS +1; HP +0.', ARRAY['classes']::text[]),
('death-and-identity', 'Death and Identity', 'everbloom-lore/lore/death-and-identity.md', '• Downed: a traveller beaten in a fight lies downed and must win a death save. • Sworn: see Borrowed Immortality. • Unsworn: death is death. A body may be raised by another for a price (50 gold), but it returns as a zombie. • Chaos: canon says a Chaos traveller who dies is delete', 'Death and Identity
• Downed: a traveller beaten in a fight lies downed and must win a death save.
• Sworn: see Borrowed Immortality.
• Unsworn: death is death. A body may be raised by another for a price (50 gold), but it returns as a zombie.
• Chaos: canon says a Chaos traveller who dies is deleted permanently: there is no one to carry them home. (Not yet enforced by the game; for now Chaos follows the unsworn rule.)', ARRAY['death and identity']::text[]),
('gold-economy', 'Gold Economy', 'everbloom-lore/lore/gold-economy.md', 'Gold is meant to move. The First City is the safest place to store it before it is committed to the world: banks there and in the towns hold coin and goods.', 'Gold Economy

Gold is meant to move. The First City is the safest place to store it before it is committed to the world: banks there and in the towns hold coin and goods.
• Markets set live prices; towns levy tariffs.
• Hoarding wakes Dragons: great stagnant hoards draw them to vaults and trade roads.
• Thalvora''s followers trade on better terms, and attract Dragons faster.
• NPCs earn wages, pay for meals and company, and pay the rewards on the jobs they post.', ARRAY['gold economy']::text[]),
('measure', 'Measure', 'everbloom-lore/lore/measure.md', 'Recorded by the Veiled, who observe it but do not judge it, with The First City as its primary ledger. Chaos players generate high, volatile Measure.', 'Measure

Recorded by the Veiled, who observe it but do not judge it, with The First City as its primary ledger. Chaos players generate high, volatile Measure.

(Stub: what Measure is, and how it is earned, is not yet written. The game does not track it yet.)', ARRAY['measure']::text[]),
('non-serviam', 'Non Serviam', 'everbloom-lore/lore/non-serviam.md', '"I will not serve." The phrase that forsakes the Aethers and binds the speaker to Chaos. Spoken at the Hidden Fissure of The First City.', 'Non Serviam

"I will not serve." The phrase that forsakes the Aethers and binds the speaker to Chaos. Spoken at the Hidden Fissure of The First City.

It is irreversible, and the whole world hears it: a ripple of events and rumours spreads from the place it is spoken. Those who speak it then walk Chaos''s own seven-step path, which ends in hunting an Aether''s Wardens.', ARRAY['non serviam']::text[]),
('otherworlders', 'Otherworlders', 'everbloom-lore/lore/otherworlders.md', 'The world''s name for travellers (the players): people who wake on the Crescent Shore, or wander in from The Wilderness, unable to recall how they came. The Veiled welcome them into The First City and see them through Character Creation at the Gate of Form.', 'Otherworlders

The world''s name for travellers (the players): people who wake on the Crescent Shore, or wander in from The Wilderness, unable to recall how they came. The Veiled welcome them into The First City and see them through Character Creation at the Gate of Form.

Because they are free to choose, the Aethers cannot command them, only court and nudge them. Some swear to an Aether; some refuse; some become Self-Formed.', ARRAY['otherworlders']::text[]),
('prehistory', 'Prehistory', 'everbloom-lore/lore/prehistory--e32509a9-6195-4129-85c0-25d64b777974.md', 'The generated history of the world before player arrival (approx. 300 years).', 'Prehistory

The generated history of the world before player arrival (approx. 300 years).

The Epochs
1.  The First Speaking: Creation of the City and Kingdoms.
2.  The Crescent Wars: Early conflicts that solidified borders.
3.  The Age of Stabilization: Rise of Guilds, Banks, and the Veiled.
4.  The Quiet Rot: Current era. Stagnation, hoarding, and the return of Dragons.

State at Launch
• All 7 Aethers are alive.
• The Crescent Kingdoms are stable but brittle.
• Lore is contradictory (different myths in different lands).', ARRAY['prehistory']::text[]),
('races', 'Races', 'everbloom-lore/lore/races.md', 'The peoples an Otherworlder may be born as at the Gate of Form (see Character Creation). Each race shifts the six ability scores from a base of 10.', 'Races

The peoples an Otherworlder may be born as at the Gate of Form (see Character Creation). Each race shifts the six ability scores from a base of 10.

Human
Adaptable wanderers from the old kingdoms. Unremarkable, and therefore everywhere.
• Gift: Balanced — gain extra starting gold and a second guess in any conversation.
• Cost: No standout edge in body or sense.
• Abilities: WIS +1, CHA +1; HP +0.

Elf
Slender folk of the long memory. They listen for things others cannot hear.
• Gift: Keen perception — see lore hints others miss; +1 to discovery checks.
• Cost: Frail frame. -3 max HP.
• Abilities: DEX +2, WIS +1, CON -1; HP -3.

Dwarf
Stone-shouldered hill folk who measure trust in decades.
• Gift: Hardy. +4 max HP and resistance to fear effects.
• Cost: Slow — initiative penalty in combat. Distrustful of magic.
• Abilities: CON +2, STR +1, CHA -1; HP +4.

Half-Giant
Children of the Whispering Wastes, where the old titans still sleep.
• Gift: Massive. +8 max HP; can shoulder doors and intimidate weaker foes.
• Cost: Conspicuous — cannot enter low buildings; merchants charge double.
• Abilities: STR +3, CON +1, DEX -1, CHA -1; HP +8.

Beastman
Pact-born of the deep wilds — wolf, bear, falcon, or feline kin.
• Gift: Senses of the wild — track scents, hear whispers; +1 to all examine actions.
• Cost: Distrusted in cities. Cultists may refuse you passage.
• Abilities: WIS +2, DEX +1, CHA -1; HP +0.

Sandborn
Desert-blooded nomads who can hear the dunes turn over in their sleep.
• Gift: Heat immunity; double water reserves; the snake of the oasis remembers your face.
• Cost: Cannot stand cold. -2 HP in any northern region.
• Abilities: CON +1, DEX +1, WIS +1, INT -1; HP +0.', ARRAY['races']::text[]),
('the-blinding-verdict', 'The Blinding Verdict', 'everbloom-lore/lore/the-blinding-verdict--db991d57-a270-4818-93f0-2ea1eb92a9de.md', 'Referred to in hushed tones among the Grand Luminares, The Blinding Verdict is a legendary event, a powerful manifestation of Solastin''s cutting truth. Lore speaks of a singular moment in Prehistory when a cabal of powerful sorcerers allied with Chaos attempted to permanently shr', 'The Blinding Verdict

Referred to in hushed tones among the Grand Luminares, The Blinding Verdict is a legendary event, a powerful manifestation of Solastin''s cutting truth. Lore speaks of a singular moment in Prehistory when a cabal of powerful sorcerers allied with Chaos attempted to permanently shroud a vast portion of The Crescent Kingdoms in illusions and falsehoods. 

In response, Solastin is said to have unleashed a devastating, purifying blast of light—a ''Blade'' made manifest—that not only shattered the illusions but also permanently stripped the cabal of their sight and their ability to lie, making them unwitting heralds of truth. The land scarred by this event, now known as the ''Scarred Peaks,'' is still considered cursed by those with deceitful hearts, and it serves as a stark reminder of the Aether''s unyielding justice. This devastating power is often compared to the destructive force of a Dragon''s fury, yet its purpose is purification rather than destruction.', ARRAY['blinding verdict', 'the blinding verdict', 'legend', 'solastin', 'divine intervention', 'chaos']::text[]),
('the-crescent-wars', 'The Crescent Wars', 'everbloom-lore/lore/the-crescent-wars.md', 'The early conflicts, in the second epoch of Prehistory, that hardened the borders of The Crescent Kingdoms and solidified each Aether''s influence over its land. Their scars still mark those borders.', 'The Crescent Wars

The early conflicts, in the second epoch of Prehistory, that hardened the borders of The Crescent Kingdoms and solidified each Aether''s influence over its land. Their scars still mark those borders.

(Stub: the owner has not yet written the history of the wars.)', ARRAY['crescent wars', 'the crescent wars']::text[]),
('the-shadow', 'The Shadow', 'everbloom-lore/lore/the-shadow.md', 'The whisper of Chaos. For every word the Speaker spoke, an unsaid thing was pushed aside; the Shadow is what was not chosen, given a hunger. It collects in the unspoken places: the open dunes, wells gone dry, rooms where an old promise was broken.', 'The Shadow

The whisper of Chaos. For every word the Speaker spoke, an unsaid thing was pushed aside; the Shadow is what was not chosen, given a hunger. It collects in the unspoken places: the open dunes, wells gone dry, rooms where an old promise was broken.

Travellers who refuse every Aether, or linger in The Wilderness, hear it offering to finish their sentences. Listening deepens their corruption; resisting pushes it back. Those who give in may speak Non Serviam.', ARRAY['shadow', 'the shadow']::text[]),
('warden-destruction-rules', 'Warden Destruction Rules', 'everbloom-lore/lore/warden-destruction-rules.md', 'The grim calculus of an Aether''s death.', 'Warden Destruction Rules

The grim calculus of an Aether''s death.

1. Each Aether is bound to twelve Wardens: people, items and places.
2. An Aether lives while any one of its Wardens still stands.
3. People are destroyed by being slain. A slain Warden never returns.
4. Items (relics) are destroyed by shattering them where they rest: a test of Strength against the relic.
5. Places are destroyed by desecrating them: a ritual test of Wisdom while standing within.
6. A failed attempt draws the Warden''s backlash and wounds the attacker. A Warden can be tested only once an hour by the same hand.
7. Those sworn to an Aether cannot raise a hand against its Wardens.
8. When the last Warden falls, the Aether desynchronizes: it falls, its kingdom drifts without its bias, and no one it once sheltered is carried home at death.', ARRAY['warden destruction rules']::text[])
